\title{Sharp First-Order Drift Laws for\\ Kullback--Leibler-Regularised Alignment}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $p$ be a strictly positive probability vector on a finite set $\Om$, let $r:\Om\to\R$ be a
reward, and let $\pi_\beta(y)=p(y)e^{r(y)/\beta}/\sum_z p(z)e^{r(z)/\beta}$ be the Gibbs
(Kullback--Leibler-regularised) policy at temperature $\beta>0$, i.e.\ the unique maximiser of
$\E_q[r]-\beta\KL(q\|p)$. We determine the exact first-order behaviour of three drift functionals
along the path $\beta\mapsto\pi_\beta$ as $\beta\to\infty$. The total-variation ($\ell^1$) drift
satisfies $\beta\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1\to\MAD_p(r):=\E_p|r-\E_p r|$ with explicit two-sided error
$O(\Var_p(r)\beta^{-2})$ valid for all $\beta\ge\operatorname{range}(r)$; the relative-entropy drift
satisfies $\beta^2\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)\to\tfrac12\Var_p(r)$; and for every statistic $f$ the audit drift
satisfies $\beta(\E_{\pi_\beta}f-\E_p f)\to\Cov_p(r,f)$. These identify the exact absolute constants
of bounds previously known only up to constants and an exponential prefactor
$e^{\operatorname{range}(r)/\beta}$, and correct the functional in the total-variation case: the
governing quantity is the mean absolute deviation, an $L^1$ moment, not the standard deviation. We
prove the deviation defect identity $\Var_p(r)-\MAD_p(r)^2=\E_p(|r-\E_p r|-\MAD_p(r))^2$, giving
$\MAD\le\sigma$ with equality iff $|r-\E_p r|$ is constant, and exhibit a rare-spike family on which
$\MAD/\sigma\to0$. Combining the first two laws evaluates the Pinsker defect along the alignment path
exactly: $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1/\sqrt{2\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)}\to\MAD_p(r)/\sigma_p(r)$, equal to $1$ precisely
for balanced two-valued rewards. The covariance law implies that an audit statistic uncorrelated with
the reward has drift $o(\beta^{-1})$: first-order reward hacking is exactly the reward-correlated
component of the statistic, and the familiar bound $\sigma_p(r)\sigma_p(f)/\beta$ is its
Cauchy--Schwarz relaxation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The setting}

Adjusting a generative model to score better on a learned reward is standardly formulated as the
Kullback--Leibler-regularised control problem
\begin{equation}\label{eq:opt}
\pi_\beta \;=\; \argmax_{q\in\Delta(\Om)}\Big\{\E_q[r]-\beta\KL(q\|p)\Big\},\qquad \beta>0,
\end{equation}
whose solution is the exponential tilt
\begin{equation}\label{eq:gibbs}
\pi_\beta(y)=\frac{p(y)e^{r(y)/\beta}}{Z_\beta},\qquad Z_\beta=\sum_z p(z)e^{r(z)/\beta}.
\end{equation}
The regime of interest is $\beta$ large: the model moves only slightly and one wants a quantitative
guarantee on how slightly. Known bounds have the shape
\begin{equation}\label{eq:classical}
\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)\le\frac{e^{R/\beta}\Var_p(r)}{\beta^2},\qquad
\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1\le\frac{\sqrt{2e^{R/\beta}\Var_p(r)}}{\beta},
\end{equation}
with $R=\operatorname{range}(r)=\max r-\min r$, together with two-point families showing the drift is
$\Theta(\sigma_p(r)/\beta)$. Three questions are left open: what is the absolute constant; is the
prefactor $e^{R/\beta}$ necessary; and is $\sigma_p(r)$ the correct functional?

\subsection{Contributions}

The prefactor is unnecessary, the constants are $1$, $\tfrac12$ and $1$, and $\sigma_p(r)$ is
\emph{not} the correct functional for total variation.
\begin{enumerate}
\item Total variation (Theorem~\ref{thm:tv}, Corollary~\ref{cor:tv}): for $\beta\ge R$,
$\MAD_p(r)/\beta-3\Var_p(r)/\beta^2\le\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1\le\MAD_p(r)/\beta+2\Var_p(r)/\beta^2$.
\item The deviation defect (Theorems~\ref{thm:defect}, \ref{thm:eqcase}): $\Var-\MAD^2$ is the
variance of the absolute deviation, so $\MAD\le\sigma$ with a sharp equality case.
\item Relative entropy (Theorem~\ref{thm:kl}, Corollary~\ref{cor:kl}): $\beta^2\KL\to\tfrac12\Var_p(r)$.
\item Audit drift (Theorem~\ref{thm:audit} and corollaries): $\beta(\E_{\pi_\beta}f-\E_pf)\to\Cov_p(r,f)$,
with first-order invariance for uncorrelated statistics.
\item Exact Pinsker defect (Theorems~\ref{thm:pinsker}, \ref{thm:pinskertight}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The organising principle}

The path $\beta\mapsto\pi_\beta$ is a reparametrised one-parameter exponential family with natural
parameter $1/\beta$ and sufficient statistic $r$. Every drift functional therefore admits a
cumulant/moment expansion in $1/\beta$, and its leading behaviour is governed by \emph{the first
cumulant or moment it does not annihilate}: total variation is an $L^1$ norm and reads $\E_p|r-\mu|$;
relative entropy is a quadratic form whose Hessian is the Fisher information and reads $\Var_p(r)$;
a linear functional reads $\Cov_p(r,f)$. The three constants in the literature ($R$, $\sigma$,
$\MAD$) are the centred $L^q(p)$ norms of the reward at $q=\infty,2,1$.

\section{Definitions and notation}

Throughout $\Om$ is a finite non-empty set.

\begin{definition}
$p:\Om\to\R$ is a \emph{distribution} if $p(y)\ge0$ for all $y$ and $\sum_yp(y)=1$; it is a
\emph{positive distribution} if moreover $p(y)>0$ for all $y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For a distribution $p$ and $f,g:\Om\to\R$,
\[
\E_p[f]=\sum_yp(y)f(y),\qquad \Var_p(f)=\sum_yp(y)\big(f(y)-\E_pf\big)^2,
\]
\[
\MAD_p(f)=\sum_yp(y)\big|f(y)-\E_pf\big|,\qquad
\Cov_p(f,g)=\sum_yp(y)\big(f(y)-\E_pf\big)\big(g(y)-\E_pg\big).
\]
We write $\sigma_p(f)=\sqrt{\Var_p(f)}$, $R(f)=\max_yf(y)-\min_yf(y)$, $R=R(r)$ and $\mu=\E_pr$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For $\beta>0$ the Gibbs policy is given by \eqref{eq:gibbs}. The divergences are
$\KL(q\|p)=\sum_yq(y)\log\frac{q(y)}{p(y)}$ and $\|q-p\|_1=\sum_y|q(y)-p(y)|$; total variation is
$\tfrac12\|q-p\|_1$ and we work with the $\ell^1$ norm.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Centred tilt]
Write $s(y)=(r(y)-\mu)/\beta$ and $W_\beta=\E_p[e^{s}]=\sum_yp(y)e^{(r(y)-\mu)/\beta}$.
\end{definition}

Since $Z_\beta=e^{\mu/\beta}W_\beta$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:centred}
\pi_\beta(y)=\frac{p(y)e^{s(y)}}{W_\beta},
\end{equation}
which is the form used exclusively below: the mean of the reward is a gauge freedom. Note $\E_p[s]=0$.

\begin{lemma}[Basic bounds]\label{lem:basic}
Let $p$ be a distribution, $\beta>0$. Then
\emph{(i)} $|r(y)-\mu|\le R$ for all $y$, hence $\MAD_p(r)\le R$;
\emph{(ii)} (Popoviciu) $\Var_p(r)\le R^2/4$;
\emph{(iii)} (Jensen) $W_\beta\ge1$;
\emph{(iv)} if $\beta\ge R$ then $W_\beta\le1+\Var_p(r)/\beta^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(i) The mean lies between $\min r$ and $\max r$. (ii) The variance is at most the mean square
deviation from any centre; take $c=\tfrac12(\max r+\min r)$, for which $(r(y)-c)^2\le R^2/4$
pointwise. (iii) $\E_pe^{s}\ge e^{\E_ps}=1$. (iv) For $|u|\le1$, $e^u\le1+u+u^2$; by (i) and
$\beta\ge R$, $|s(y)|\le1$, so $W_\beta\le1+\E_ps+\E_ps^2=1+\Var_p(r)/\beta^2$.
\end{proof}

We use repeatedly the Taylor bound $|e^u-1-u|\le u^2$ for $|u|\le1$.

\section{The deviation defect: $\MAD$ versus $\sigma$}

\begin{theorem}[Deviation defect identity]\label{thm:defect}
For any distribution $p$ and any $f:\Om\to\R$,
\[
\Var_p(f)-\MAD_p(f)^2=\sum_yp(y)\Big(\big|f(y)-\E_pf\big|-\MAD_p(f)\Big)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Put $a(y)=|f(y)-\E_pf|$ and $m=\E_p[a]=\MAD_p(f)$. Then
$\E_p[(a-m)^2]=\E_p[a^2]-2m\E_p[a]+m^2=\E_p[a^2]-m^2$, and $a(y)^2=(f(y)-\E_pf)^2$, so
$\E_p[a^2]=\Var_p(f)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:madle}
$\MAD_p(f)^2\le\Var_p(f)$, i.e.\ $\MAD_p(f)\le\sigma_p(f)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Equality case]\label{thm:eqcase}
Let $p$ be a positive distribution. Then $\MAD_p(f)=\sigma_p(f)$ if and only if
$|f(y)-\E_pf|=\MAD_p(f)$ for every $y$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equality in Corollary~\ref{cor:madle} forces the sum in Theorem~\ref{thm:defect} to vanish; since
$p(y)>0$, every summand vanishes. Conversely, if the absolute deviation is constant the defect is
zero, so $\MAD^2=\Var$, and both quantities are non-negative.
\end{proof}

Equality therefore holds precisely for \emph{balanced two-valued} statistics: $f$ takes two values
placed symmetrically about its mean (or is constant, both sides being zero).

\begin{example}[Rare spike]\label{ex:spike}
Let $\Om=\{0,1\}$, $p(1)=\varepsilon\in(0,1)$, $r=\mathbf 1_{\{1\}}$. Then $\E_pr=\varepsilon$ and
\[
\MAD_p(r)=2\varepsilon(1-\varepsilon),\qquad \Var_p(r)=\varepsilon(1-\varepsilon),\qquad
\MAD_p(r)^2=4\varepsilon(1-\varepsilon)\Var_p(r),
\]
so $\MAD_p(r)/\sigma_p(r)=2\sqrt{\varepsilon(1-\varepsilon)}\to0$ as $\varepsilon\to0$, while at
$\varepsilon=\tfrac12$ the ratio equals $1$.
\end{example}

Hence the $\sigma/\beta$ law is never violated by the sharp $\MAD/\beta$ law, is attained exactly on
the balanced two-valued family --- precisely the family previously used to show the $\sigma$-law
could not be improved by more than a constant --- and is unboundedly lossy on rare-spike rewards.

\section{The total-variation drift law}

\begin{lemma}[Centred $\ell^1$ identity]\label{lem:l1id}
For a positive distribution $p$ and $\beta>0$,
\[
\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1=\frac{S_\beta}{W_\beta},\qquad S_\beta:=\sum_yp(y)\big|e^{s(y)}-W_\beta\big| .
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:centred}, $\pi_\beta(y)-p(y)=p(y)(e^{s(y)}-W_\beta)/W_\beta$; take absolute values and
sum, using $p(y)>0$ and $W_\beta>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Numerator expansion]\label{lem:l1num}
If $p$ is a distribution, $\beta>0$ and $\beta\ge R$, then
$\big|S_\beta-\MAD_p(r)/\beta\big|\le 2\Var_p(r)/\beta^2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Pointwise, $\big||e^{s}-W_\beta|-|s|\big|\le|e^{s}-W_\beta-s|\le|e^{s}-1-s|+|W_\beta-1|$. The first
term is at most $s^2$ by the Taylor bound (valid since $|s|\le R/\beta\le1$), the second at most
$\Var_p(r)/\beta^2$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}(iii)--(iv), uniformly in $y$. Take $\E_p$ and use
$\E_p|s|=\MAD_p(r)/\beta$ and $\E_p[s^2]=\Var_p(r)/\beta^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp two-sided drift law]\label{thm:tv}
Let $p$ be a positive distribution and $\beta>0$ with $\beta\ge R$. Then
\[
\frac{\MAD_p(r)}{\beta}-\frac{3\Var_p(r)}{\beta^2}\;\le\;\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1\;\le\;
\frac{\MAD_p(r)}{\beta}+\frac{2\Var_p(r)}{\beta^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Upper bound.} By Lemma~\ref{lem:l1id} and $W_\beta\ge1$, $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1\le S_\beta$, and
Lemma~\ref{lem:l1num} bounds $S_\beta$.

\emph{Lower bound.} Put $X=\MAD_p(r)/\beta-2\Var_p(r)/\beta^2$, so $S_\beta\ge X$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:l1num}. If $X\le0$ the claim is trivial. If $X>0$ then, using $W_\beta\ge1$ and
$X/W\ge X-X(W-1)$ for $W\ge1$, $X>0$,
\[
\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1=\frac{S_\beta}{W_\beta}\ge\frac{X}{W_\beta}\ge X-X(W_\beta-1).
\]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}(i) and $\beta\ge R$ we have $X\le\MAD_p(r)/\beta\le R/\beta\le1$, and by
Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}(iv) $W_\beta-1\le\Var_p(r)/\beta^2$; hence $X(W_\beta-1)\le\Var_p(r)/\beta^2$
and the total error constant is $2+1=3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact drift constant]\label{cor:tv}
$\displaystyle\lim_{\beta\to\infty}\beta\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1=\MAD_p(r)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Multiply the bounds of Theorem~\ref{thm:tv} by $\beta$; both envelopes are $\MAD_p(r)+O(\beta^{-1})$
and the hypothesis $\beta\ge R$ holds eventually. Squeeze.
\end{proof}

No exponential prefactor appears, and the leading constant is exactly $1$. With
Corollary~\ref{cor:madle} this shows the classical $\sigma$-law is a valid upper bound;
Example~\ref{ex:spike} shows the loss can be arbitrarily large.

\section{The relative-entropy drift law}

Define the tilted first moment $A_\beta=\E_p[e^{s}s]$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Apos}
$A_\beta\ge0$ for every distribution $p$ and every $\beta$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Since $\E_p[s]=0$, $A_\beta=\E_p[(e^{s}-1)s]$, and $(e^u-1)u\ge0$ for all real $u$ because $e^u-1$
has the sign of $u$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exact centred KL identity]\label{lem:klid}
For a positive distribution $p$ and $\beta>0$,
$\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)=A_\beta/W_\beta-\log W_\beta$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:centred}, $\pi_\beta(y)/p(y)=e^{s(y)}/W_\beta$, so
$\log(\pi_\beta(y)/p(y))=s(y)-\log W_\beta$, and
\[
\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)=\sum_y\frac{p(y)e^{s(y)}}{W_\beta}\big(s(y)-\log W_\beta\big)
=\frac{A_\beta}{W_\beta}-\log W_\beta. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Second-order KL law]\label{thm:kl}
Let $p$ be a positive distribution and $\beta>0$ with $\beta\ge R$. Then, writing $V=\Var_p(r)$,
\[
\Big|\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)-\frac{V}{2\beta^2}\Big|\;\le\;\frac{2RV}{\beta^3}+\frac{3V^2}{\beta^4}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Three expansions feed Lemma~\ref{lem:klid}.
\emph{(1) First moment:} $|A_\beta-V/\beta^2|\le\E_p|e^{s}-1-s|\,|s|\le\E_p|s|^3\le(R/\beta)V/\beta^2$.
\emph{(2) Partition function:} $|W_\beta-1-V/(2\beta^2)|\le\E_p|e^{s}-1-s-s^2/2|\le\E_p|s|^3
\le(R/\beta)V/\beta^2$, using $\E_ps=0$.
\emph{(3) Logarithm:} for $W\ge1$, $W-1-(W-1)^2\le\log W\le W-1$, and $0\le W_\beta-1\le V/\beta^2$
by Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}, so $(W_\beta-1)^2\le V^2/\beta^4$.
Moreover $A_\beta/W_\beta$ differs from $A_\beta$ by at most $A_\beta(W_\beta-1)\le2(V/\beta^2)^2$,
using $A_\beta\le2V/\beta^2$ from (1) with $R\le\beta$. Substituting into Lemma~\ref{lem:klid},
\[
\KL=A_\beta-\log W_\beta+O(V^2\beta^{-4})=\frac{V}{\beta^2}-\frac{V}{2\beta^2}+O(RV\beta^{-3})
+O(V^2\beta^{-4}),
\]
and collecting the explicit constants gives $2RV/\beta^3+3V^2/\beta^4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:kl}
$\displaystyle\lim_{\beta\to\infty}\beta^2\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)=\tfrac12\Var_p(r)$, hence
$\lim_{\beta\to\infty}\beta\sqrt{2\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)}=\sigma_p(r)$.
\end{corollary}

The one half is exactly the cancellation between the first-moment term $V/\beta^2$ and the
log-partition term $V/(2\beta^2)$; equivalently, the Fisher information of $\{\pi_\beta\}$ in the
natural parameter $\theta=1/\beta$ equals $\Var_p(r)$ at $\theta=0$, and relative entropy is one half
the Fisher quadratic form to leading order.

\section{The audit-drift law: the constant is the covariance}

Let $f:\Om\to\R$ be an arbitrary \emph{audit statistic}.

\begin{lemma}[Exact audit identity]\label{lem:auditid}
For a positive distribution $p$ and $\beta>0$,
\[
\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]=\frac{1}{W_\beta}\sum_yp(y)\big(e^{s(y)}-1\big)\big(f(y)-\E_pf\big).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:centred}, $\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]=\E_p[e^{s}f]/W_\beta$. Expanding,
$\E_p[(e^{s}-1)(f-\E_pf)]=\E_p[e^{s}f]-\E_pf\cdot W_\beta$, and dividing by $W_\beta$ gives the
claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Numerator expansion]\label{lem:auditnum}
If $p$ is a distribution, $\beta>0$ and $\beta\ge R$, then
\[
\Big|\sum_yp(y)\big(e^{s(y)}-1\big)\big(f(y)-\E_pf\big)-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}\Big|
\le\frac{R(f)\Var_p(r)}{\beta^2}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta=\E_p[s(f-\E_pf)]$. Subtracting termwise and using $|e^{s}-1-s|\le s^2$ (valid as
$|s|\le1$) and $|f(y)-\E_pf|\le R(f)$, the difference is at most $R(f)\E_p[s^2]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The audit-drift constant is the covariance]\label{thm:audit}
Let $p$ be a positive distribution and $\beta>0$ with $\beta\ge R$. Then for every $f$,
\[
\Big|\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}\Big|\;\le\;\frac{3R(f)\Var_p(r)}{\beta^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $D$ be the numerator of Lemma~\ref{lem:auditid}. Then
\[
\E_{\pi_\beta}f-\E_pf-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}
=\Big(D-\frac{\Cov_p(r,f)}{\beta}\Big)-D\,\frac{W_\beta-1}{W_\beta}.
\]
The first bracket is at most $R(f)V/\beta^2$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:auditnum}. For the second,
$|\Cov_p(r,f)|\le R\,R(f)$ by the pointwise bounds $|r-\mu|\le R$ and $|f-\E_pf|\le R(f)$, so
$|\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta|\le R(f)$ when $\beta\ge R$; combined with Lemma~\ref{lem:auditnum} and
$V/\beta^2\le1$ (from Popoviciu, $V\le R^2/4\le\beta^2$) this gives $|D|\le2R(f)$. Since
$1\le W_\beta\le1+V/\beta^2$, $|D|(W_\beta-1)/W_\beta\le2R(f)V/\beta^2$. Adding the two contributions
gives the constant $3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Audit limit law]\label{cor:audit}
$\displaystyle\lim_{\beta\to\infty}\beta\big(\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f]\big)=\Cov_p(r,f)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Uncorrelated statistics are first-order unhackable]\label{cor:unhack}
If $\Cov_p(r,f)=0$ then $\beta(\E_{\pi_\beta}[f]-\E_p[f])\to0$; the drift of $f$ is $o(\beta^{-1})$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The standard anti-reward-hacking guarantee has the form
$|\E_{\pi_\beta}f-\E_pf|\le\sigma_p(r)\sigma_p(f)e^{R/\beta}/\beta$. Theorem~\ref{thm:audit}
identifies its content exactly: the true first-order coefficient is $\Cov_p(r,f)$, and
$|\Cov_p(r,f)|\le\sigma_p(r)\sigma_p(f)$ is Cauchy--Schwarz. The bound is tight exactly when $f$ is
an affine function of $r$ and arbitrarily loose otherwise --- infinitely loose for uncorrelated $f$.
\end{remark}

\section{The exact Pinsker defect along the alignment path}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sqrtkl}
$\beta\sqrt{2\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)}\to\sigma_p(r)$ as $\beta\to\infty$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\beta\sqrt{2\KL}=\sqrt{2\beta^2\KL}\to\sqrt{2\cdot V/2}=\sigma_p(r)$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:kl} and
continuity of the square root.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact Pinsker defect]\label{thm:pinsker}
Let $p$ be a positive distribution with $\Var_p(r)>0$. Then
\[
\lim_{\beta\to\infty}\frac{\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1}{\sqrt{2\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)}}
=\frac{\MAD_p(r)}{\sigma_p(r)}\;\le\;1.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Multiply numerator and denominator by $\beta$ and apply Corollary~\ref{cor:tv} and
Lemma~\ref{lem:sqrtkl}; the denominator limit is non-zero by hypothesis. The inequality is
Corollary~\ref{cor:madle}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic tightness of Pinsker]\label{thm:pinskertight}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:pinsker}, the limiting ratio equals $1$ if and only if
$|r(y)-\E_pr|=\MAD_p(r)$ for every $y$, i.e.\ exactly for balanced two-valued rewards.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The ratio is $\MAD_p(r)/\sigma_p(r)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:pinsker}, which equals $1$ iff
$\MAD_p(r)=\sigma_p(r)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:eqcase}.
\end{proof}

Thus the standard-deviation constant of the classical drift law is exactly the Pinsker relaxation of
the true total-variation constant, and the amount lost is the deviation defect
$\sigma_p(r)-\MAD_p(r)$.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Gibbs tilt in centred form.}
Given $p,r,\beta$: compute $\mu=\sum_yp(y)r(y)$; form $w(y)=\exp((r(y)-\mu)/\beta)$; set
$W=\sum_yp(y)w(y)$; output $\pi(y)=p(y)w(y)/W$. Centring keeps the exponent in $[-1,1]$ whenever
$\beta\ge R$, giving numerical stability throughout the regime of interest. Cost $O(|\Om|)$.

\paragraph{B. Drift profile.}
Over a grid of temperatures, evaluate $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1$, $\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)$ and
$\E_{\pi_\beta}f-\E_pf$ together with the rescalings $\beta\|\cdot\|_1$, $\beta^2\KL$ and
$\beta\times$ audit gap, and compare with $\MAD_p(r)$, $\tfrac12\Var_p(r)$ and $\Cov_p(r,f)$.
Residuals decay like $\beta^{-1}$. Cost $O(|\Om|)$ per temperature.

\paragraph{C. Temperature budgeting.}
For a tolerance $\delta>0$, solving $\MAD_p(r)/\beta+2\Var_p(r)/\beta^2=\delta$ gives
\[
\beta^\star=\frac{\MAD_p(r)+\sqrt{\MAD_p(r)^2+8\delta\Var_p(r)}}{2\delta},
\]
clipped below at $R(r)$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:tv} this certifies $\|\pi_{\beta^\star}-p\|_1\le\delta$.
Both moments are computable in $O(|\Om|)$, so the certificate requires no tuning.

\paragraph{D. Audit-metric screening.}
For candidates $f_1,\dots,f_k$, compute $c_i=\Cov_p(r,f_i)$ and rank by $|c_i|$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:audit} the drift of $f_i$ is $c_i/\beta\pm3R(f_i)\Var_p(r)/\beta^2$; statistics with
$c_i=0$ are first-order invariant. Cost $O(k|\Om|)$.

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\paragraph{Certified drift budgets.}
To guarantee total-variation change at most $\delta$, take $\beta\approx\MAD_p(r)/\delta$. Bounds of
the form \eqref{eq:classical} demand $\beta\approx\sqrt2\,\sigma_p(r)e^{R/2\beta}/\delta$,
over-provisioning by $\sqrt2\,\sigma/\MAD$, which Example~\ref{ex:spike} shows is unbounded: on a
reward firing with probability $10^{-6}$ the factor exceeds $700$.

\paragraph{Reward-shape diagnostics.}
The number $\MAD_p(r)/\sigma_p(r)\in(0,1]$ classifies rewards: near $1$ the signal is balanced and
essentially binary; near $0$ it is a rare spike concentrating its variance on a low-probability set.
By Theorem~\ref{thm:pinsker} the same number is the asymptotic slack of Pinsker's inequality along
the alignment path.

\paragraph{Audit design.}
Corollary~\ref{cor:unhack} turns metric selection into linear algebra: project the candidate
statistic onto the reward in $L^2(p)$. The orthogonal component is first-order invariant; the
parallel component drifts at rate $\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta$. A large $|\Cov_p(r,f)|$ for an undesirable $f$
is a quantitative, pre-optimization red flag.

\paragraph{Interpretation of reward hacking.}
In the weak-alignment regime the drift of any statistic is exactly its covariance with the reward,
divided by $\beta$. What makes hacking surprising in practice is not the mechanism but the estimation
problem: the covariance is taken under the base policy, and rare high-reward regions contribute in
ways finite samples underestimate.

\section{Discussion and limitations}

The results are exact but asymptotic in $\beta$, with explicit errors valid non-asymptotically for
$\beta\ge R(r)$. Three limitations deserve emphasis.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Finite outcome space.} All statements are for finite $\Om$. The proofs use only Taylor
bounds, Jensen's inequality and finite sums, so they extend to any base measure with bounded $r$;
unbounded sub-Gaussian rewards should admit the same laws with $R$ replaced by a tail parameter, but
that is not proved here.
\item \emph{The regime $\beta\ge R$.} Below the reward range the tilt is not a perturbation and the
expansions fail; there the drift saturates, with $\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1\to2(1-p(\argmax r))$ as
$\beta\to0$ for a unique maximiser. The transition between linear response and concentration is not
analysed.
\item \emph{Population, not sample.} The moments are taken under the exact base policy. The $L^1$
functional $\MAD$ has different estimation behaviour from the variance, and sharp laws for estimated
moments are open.
\end{enumerate}

Conceptually, the corrected functional is not an accident of total variation being unusual. It
follows from a general principle: the leading drift constant of a divergence is the first cumulant or
moment of the reward that the divergence does not annihilate. Total variation is homogeneous of
degree $1$ in the perturbation and reads an $L^1$ moment; relative entropy is degree $2$ and reads a
second cumulant; linear functionals read the mixed second moment. Any law that assigns the same
constant to all three conflates the divergence with the statistic.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{D1. R\'enyi-order interpolation.}
For $\alpha\in(0,\infty)$ we conjecture $D_\alpha(\pi_\beta\|p)=\alpha\Var_p(r)/(2\beta^2)
+O(\beta^{-3})$, and $\|(\pi_\beta-p)/p\|_{L^q(p)}=\|r-\E_pr\|_{L^q(p)}/\beta+O(\beta^{-2})$ for all
$q\in[1,\infty]$. The family of drift constants would then be the family of centred $L^q$ norms of
the reward: $q=1$ gives $\MAD$ (total variation), $q=2$ gives $\sigma$ ($\chi^2$/Pinsker), $q=\infty$
gives the range --- endpoints and midpoint of one interpolation family indexed by divergence order.

\paragraph{D2. Higher-order expansions.}
The next term in each law should be a third cumulant: skewness for KL, and for total variation a term
involving $\E_p[(r-\mu)^2\operatorname{sgn}(r-\mu)]$. This would upgrade the two-sided bounds to
asymptotic series and turn the budgeting rule into a calibration.

\paragraph{D3. Beyond bounded rewards and finiteness.}
Extend to sub-Gaussian rewards on general measurable spaces. The centred-tilt identities
(Lemmas~\ref{lem:l1id}, \ref{lem:klid}, \ref{lem:auditid}) are measure-theoretic and survive; only
the Taylor control needs a new argument.

\paragraph{D4. Sequential alignment.}
For autoregressive policies with trajectory-level rewards, does the drift of a per-token statistic
decompose into a sum of per-step covariances? A chain-rule version of Corollary~\ref{cor:audit} would
give token-level attribution of alignment drift.

\paragraph{D5. Estimation theory.}
Given $n$ samples from $p$ and noisy reward evaluations, what is the minimax rate for estimating
$\MAD_p(r)$ and $\Cov_p(r,f)$, and how does estimation error propagate into $\beta^\star$?

\section{Summary}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Functional & First-order law & Explicit error ($\beta\ge R$) \\
\midrule
$\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1$ & $\MAD_p(r)/\beta$ & $-3V/\beta^2\le\cdot\le+2V/\beta^2$ \\
$\KL(\pi_\beta\|p)$ & $\Var_p(r)/(2\beta^2)$ & $2RV/\beta^3+3V^2/\beta^4$ \\
$\E_{\pi_\beta}f-\E_pf$ & $\Cov_p(r,f)/\beta$ & $3R(f)V/\beta^2$ \\
$\|\pi_\beta-p\|_1/\sqrt{2\KL}$ & $\MAD_p(r)/\sigma_p(r)$ & tight iff $|r-\mu|$ constant \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\noindent with $V=\Var_p(r)$ and $R=R(r)$. Structurally, $\MAD_p(r)\le\sigma_p(r)\le R(r)/2$, the
first with equality iff $|r-\mu|$ is constant (Theorem~\ref{thm:eqcase}), the second by Popoviciu
(Lemma~\ref{lem:basic}(ii)); and $\Var_p(r)-\MAD_p(r)^2$ is the variance of the absolute deviation
(Theorem~\ref{thm:defect}).

